#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134913} :activity/id #uuid "4e862272-bb3b-5ed1-8f65-38280b886801" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050165 :topic/id #uuid "3340d572-01cb-58fc-bf2a-d26fbeb514db" :topic/math-academy-id 2142} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135158 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 2xz  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b00702b941e40061ff242e63c0d1b33642cce9aefc1ffa9f88e9d94cd02897a.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $R$ is a type III region. So, our first task is to describe this region fully.\n\nWe start by drawing an arrow parallel to the $y$-axis that \n\n- enters the region through the *left* surface $y=0,$ and\n\n- leave the region through the *right* surface $y=6-x-z,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5102f786fa91eb862fd3f5ef8f0b2cbc85fbdff6fc616f86f498aa0da8088f14.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30f320f2e77c95c13e59bc3fdd6b273d1ad367018af3cd12bd6d2fde7cc09132.png)\n\nTo evaluate our triple integral, we start by expressing it as a mixed integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, since the region $D$ is a type I *plane* region, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand therefore, our mixed integral can be written as the following repeating integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_0^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n=\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V = 15.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d23f432-e419-54d8-8402-3f8870bb1f26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7211 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "731db301-f6e1-5bd0-b523-4fd809c75a75" :step/math-academy-id 42947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133479 :question/id #uuid "1b09be02-1515-5277-bac6-9289b9dbe371" :question/math-academy-id "e-11506"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40486856-137f-55c4-8464-f99e7e1c659f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527962}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Mixed Integral"} :step/id #uuid "cfb4ae8a-e968-506a-b418-11ac44fb550e" :step/math-academy-id 42948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135159 :tutorial/content "When writing down a triple integral as a repeated integral, we can often skip the intermediate step of expressing our triple integral as a mixed integral.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}a} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{red}b}, \\: {\\color{blue}v_1(x)} \\leq z \\leq {\\color{blue}v_2(x)}, \\: {\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane is a type II plane region. Therefore, we can immediately express our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{red}b} \\int_{\\color{blue}v_1(x)}^{\\color{blue}v_2(x)} \\int_{\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)}^{\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2d11c1f3-fd1d-51f3-81cb-0d47276b2d3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7212 :tutorial/title "Skipping the Intermediate Step"} :step/id #uuid "01b92e56-2446-57c4-b9d1-8c1de25681e2" :step/math-academy-id 42949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133480 :question/id #uuid "216b16aa-d279-519e-a749-b9fd5517a6db" :question/math-academy-id "e-11507"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf29db1b-f47b-53de-b31d-e4da1d0a9e5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050197}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528008}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"} :step/id #uuid "4ddebb29-1fb3-53f4-806a-64cc665ddd73" :step/math-academy-id 42950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133483 :question/id #uuid "72b1ac41-be39-5a51-bc0c-738d36cdb81b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12378"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4693bb81-f299-50fa-9cbd-3a5bb1c90da9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528006}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type III Region"} :step/id #uuid "2fbfe60b-9df5-54c5-91a0-39a1c5d11467" :step/math-academy-id 44403 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133485 :question/id #uuid "21fdb746-8831-5b97-acf0-5a14a2e34980" :question/math-academy-id "e-12400"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1889f67-327c-5e6e-b646-5bd58a244778" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527974}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Evaluating Triple Integrals Over Type III Regions From Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "42e6e990-20bd-50e0-ad23-337c1d99c6be" :step/math-academy-id 44514}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals Over Type III Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134015}]}
